<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toledan Tables</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Toledan Tables were astronomical tables compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in the later 11th century by scholars led by al-Zarqali (Arzachel) under the patronage of al-Mamun. Used to predict the positions of the Sun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a> and planets, they were translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in the 12th century and widely used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonsine-tables">Alfonsine Tables</a> replaced them in the late 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toledan-tables.jpg" alt="Toledan Tables" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerardus_Cremonensis_(1114-1187)_Wellcome_L0070081.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
